# WiFi coexistence (docs/pageflip.md section 6), LEFT half: it opens the book with WiFi already up,
# so the pair must not form at all -- and then WiFi goes away and it must form after all.
#
# The suspension is not politeness. The SDK transport's begin() runs its own end() first, and that
# end() disconnects WiFi and puts the radio mode back to off: a book opened during a download would
# kill the download. And ESP-NOW peers must share a channel, which an AP association chooses.
#
# `wifi on` before the book opens, because the link is brought up by the reader's onEnter. On real
# hardware you get here by joining a network from Settings and then opening a book: that path leaves
# WiFi up, unlike the seven network activities that reboot on the way out.
log pageflip-wifi-left: start
expect_timeout 90000
expect Entering activity: Home
wait 800
wifi on
tap confirm
expect Entering activity: EpubReader
# First open builds the section cache. The suspended notice comes and goes inside this window.
wait 10000
screenshot /screenshots/pf-wifi-left-00.bmp
# One press, one page: suspended is solo, not half-paired. Measured against the paired-alone
# reference, because a configured pair with no link carries the status-bar badge.
tap down
wait 3000
screenshot /screenshots/pf-wifi-left-01.bmp
log pageflip-wifi-left: releasing the radio
wifi off
# The link comes up on the next pump, greets, and the join classifies the pair. This half is a page
# ahead of the other, so section 4.2 calls it Swapped and the two exchange positions: this one lands
# back on page 0 and the other on page 1. The badge goes with the suspension.
wait 9000
screenshot /screenshots/pf-wifi-left-02.bmp
# And the pair advances by two from there.
tap down
wait 4000
screenshot /screenshots/pf-wifi-left-03.bmp
wait 1000
log pageflip-wifi-left: done
quit
